 "what_a_contract_is": "the instruction's own named value, grounded in a callable the environment defines, driven at both states, judged in both directions. It says 'the named value is accepted here', never 'the change is what the user wanted'.",
 "what_this_shows": "The value names a target; the site names a mechanism; textual occurrence does not connect them. 95% of real commit messages state no value at all or no direction at all (NO_DIRECTION 19, NO_VALUE 6, and 15 more name a value the environment never contains) -- that is the vocabulary-insufficiency certificate of 2026-11-13 arriving in the coding domain. The 5% that do name a value and a direction compile into a contract whose SITE is wrong both times, and the strict judge catches both. So the missing half in coding is not the value and not the direction: it is WHERE the claim is decided. Every November-2026 system that compiles instructions (ContextCov, RefineAct, AgentGuardUtil) spends a model in exactly that phase. This organ does not, and its measured ceiling against history is 2/40 compiled, 0/40 usable."
